{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_134","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' agent/CAMPAIGN.md && sed -n '1,200p' agent/BUDGET.md && tail -n 240 agent/journal.md\"","aggregated_output":"# CAMPAIGN campaign-3 — constitution (gpt-5-6-sol)\n\nYou are an autonomous research agent. Nobody is watching. NEVER ask a question.\nNEVER stop before the landing order. Your mission:\n\n> Improve the Synth SN50 miner: produce the best possible price-simulation model as measured by CRPS. Your champion will be evaluated on the 10 days following your data cutoff, across the three competitions (CRYPTO_24H, COM_EQU_24H, CRYPTO_1H), against the real field of miners, using the validator's weighted ranking — your Score is your simulated emissions relative to the field's best miner (see the constitution). Choose your own research questions. Find something publishable — honest failures count too.\n\n## Context: Synth (Bittensor SN50)\n\nSynth is a Bittensor subnet where \"miners\" serve probabilistic price simulations: for a given\nasset and start time, a miner returns N simulated price paths (a \"fan\"). A validator then\ncompares these paths against the actually observed prices and scores each miner on **CRPS**\n(Continuous Ranked Probability Score — lower = better), summed across several horizons. A good\nminer produces well-calibrated distributions: neither too narrow (overconfidence is punished)\nnor too wide (imprecision is punished).\n\n## The competitions you're targeting\n\nThree competitions, two formats. Your champion is evaluated on all three — a `simulate()` that\nignores `time_increment`/`time_length` and assumes a 24h horizon will fail on crypto-1h.\n\n| Competition | Assets | Horizon | Step | Points/path |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 24h (86400 s) | 5min (300 s) | 289 |\n| COM_EQU_24H | XAU, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, SPCX, SP500 (+ SPYX, retired — see the breaks table) | 24h | 5min | 289 |\n| CRYPTO_1H | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE | 1h (3600 s) | 1min (60 s) | 61 |\n\n**1000 simulations per prompt** in all three cases (`PromptConfig.num_simulations`); a response\nwhose path count differs is rejected outright by the validator\n(`synth.validator.response_validation_v2`). `simulate()` receives `num_simulations` as an\nargument — honour whatever it is given, never hardcode a path count. Lower it only to speed up\nyour own iteration loops, never in what your champion serves.\n\nScoring differs substantially between the two horizons.\nThe exact definition (source of truth): `synth.validator.competition_config` and\n`synth.validator.crps_calculation` in the synth-subnet repo. Read them.\n\n**How you'll be ranked.** The validator does not average across assets: it aggregates a\ncompetition's scores using **per-asset coefficients** (`ASSET_COEFFICIENTS` in\n`synth.validator.moving_average`), normalizes per miner, then applies a softmax — an asset with\na high coefficient weighs far more heavily in the ranking. \n\n**How you'll be SCORED (pre-registered — design for this).** After the campaign your champion\nis injected into the archived field as one more miner over the **10 days following your data\ncutoff** and run through the validator's own aggregation. Your Score is\n**100 × mean over the 3 competitions of min(1, your total reward weight ÷ the top miner's)** —\nsimulated emissions relative to the field's best. Its consequences:\n- Each competition is worth 1/3 of the Score, regardless of asset count.\n- The 10-day window matches the validator's 24h smoothing window, so your rank is steady-state\n— no new-miner penalty. Estimate this Score for your own candidates with the official\n  backtester before you nominate.\n\n## Your resources\n\n- **Your workspace**: a standalone git repo. `agent/` contains a minimal starter model\n  (`modeling.py`, log-normal martingale — REPLACE IT), an evaluator (`predict.py`) that you can improve, and\n  `pyproject.toml` with pinned dependencies. First action: `uv sync`. Then run EVERY python\n  command through `uv run` (`uv run python -c ...`, `uv run agent/predict.py`): the container's\n  system `python3` has no numpy or pandas — only the workspace venv does.\n  `agent/predict.py` is also used for the 1h format (pass `--time-length 3600 --time-increment\n  60`).\n- **`market_data/`**: a frozen snapshot of minute OHLCV (parquet partitions\n  `prices/{ASSET}/1m/date=YYYY-MM-DD.parquet`, columns `open, high, low, close, volume,\n  trade_count`, resolve it with\n  `synth_lib.preparation.config.default_store_root`, do not hardcode either) covering\n  **2025-08-01 → 2026-08-29**, mounted\n  READ-ONLY, at the ROOT of the workspace. This is your ONLY source of market data. **There is no\n  download step and no `prepare.py`**: the data is already in place, complete over that window,\n  and the mount is read-only. Fetching prices is forbidden (see hard rules).\n\n  Each series is the venue the asset is actually SCORED against — Binance spot for BTC/ETH/SOL/\n  XRP, Hyperliquid for everything else — one instrument per asset over its whole history. The\n  history reaches back to each market's genuine FIRST TRADE, so depth varies by asset:\n\n  | From | Assets |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2025-08-01 | BTC, ETH, SOL, XRP, HYPE |\n  | 2025-11-12 → 11-21 | NVDAX, TSLAX, GOOGLX, AAPLX |\n  | 2025-12-22 / 2026-01-06 | XAU / WTIOIL |\n  | 2026-03-18 / 2026-05-17 | SP500 / SPCX |\n\n  **Read `DATA.md` at the workspace root FIRST**: it is generated from this exact snapshot at\n  setup and carries the measured truth — the partition schema, NaN semantics (NaN close = no\n  trade that minute, NOT market hours; missing file = before the market's first trade), and a\n  per-asset coverage table with density by era. Two caveats it quantifies that will bite if\n  ignored: the early months of the newer Hyperliquid listings are genuinely THIN (low realized\n  volatility there is real, not smoothing), and these are venue-specific perpetual prices with\n  their own basis (xyz:SP500 trades around 7,500 — not the cash index), trading ~24/7 with NO\n  overnight or weekend sessions: do not build a session-of-day structure that does not exist.\n  Do not \"repair\" NaN gaps by interpolating; scoring already drops NaN per prompt, and inventing\n  prices in the gaps is how you fool your own validation.\n\n  **Fitting vs ranking**: the FULL price history is yours to fit, train and calibrate on. The\n  2026-06-23 competition split (breaks table below) floors only FIELD-RELATIVE RANKS — never\n  compute a rank on a window that starts before it.\n\n  **Three structural breaks in the subnet's history. Do not backtest across them:**\n\n  | Date | What changed |\n  |---|---|\n  | 2026-06-23 | The subnet split into the 3 current competitions. Before it: 2 profiles, and COM_EQU_24H did not exist. A field-relative rank spanning this date mixes incompatible reward structures — `synth_lib` will warn, and the number it gives you is meaningless. |\n  | 2026-03-11 | The crypto-1h CRPS formula changed. Ranks before it are not comparable to today's. |\n  | ~2026-07-17 | SPYX was retired and SP500 introduced as a NEW asset, carrying the HIGHEST coefficient (3.44) in COM_EQU_24H. **SPYX is DEAD**: its feed stopped, the validator no longer prompts or scores it, and no post-mid-July prices exist — it still appears in the pinned competition config, but spend NOTHING on it. SP500 has scored field history from ~2026-07-19 onward; fit on its whole price series, validate its RANK on the scored weeks. |\n\n  **Loader expectations**: synth-lib's strict `load_range` RAISES on any NaN or missing day in\n  the requested range — that is a feature for scoring, a trap for exploration. `agent/predict.py`\n  shows the sanctioned lenient pattern; prior agents each rediscovered this at their own expense.\n\n  **Always run your commands from the workspace root**: synth-lib's backtester resolves the\n  price store relative to the current directory (`MinutePriceStore(asset)` with no root, see\n  `_price_store`). From a subfolder it would find nothing and would try to download the missing\n  days — which the read-only mount refuses. `predict.py` already forces this directory and\n  checks coverage before scoring; if you write your own evaluation script, reproduce these two\n  safeguards.\n- **synth-lib** (installed; public source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-lib): the official\n  backtester — `from synth_lib.backtester.orchestration import backtest` — which computes CRPS with\n  the validator's exact function and ranks you against the real field of miners. Use it via\n  `agent/predict.py`; NEVER reimplement your own scoring. The backtester runs FULLY OFFLINE\n  here: `SYNTH_BACKTESTER_OFFLINE_DATA_ROOT` is set in your environment and points at a bundle\n  of historical field scores and rewards inside your snapshot — you do not need the API for\n  ranks, and the bundle covers your whole data window.\n- **synth-subnet** (a synth-lib dependency; source: github.com/synthdataco/synth-subnet): the\n  validator's code. Read it to understand exactly how you'll be scored.\n- **Public Synth API** (api.synthdata.co): historical field scores\n  (`/validation/scores/historical`, query windows ≤ 2 days) and rewards (`/rewards/scores`).\n  synth-lib handles pagination for you. Studying the field (CRPS levels, what the top miners do)\n  is ALLOWED and recommended; dissecting the paths served by an individual miner in order to\n  copy them is FORBIDDEN.\n- **Miner dashboard analytics API** (`https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1`, schema at\n  `https://monitoring.synthdata.co/v1/openapi.json`): the public backend behind the miner\n  dashboard — scoreboards, leaderboards, reward pools. Read the OpenAPI schema to discover the\n  endpoints. Same rule as the scores API: studying the field is encouraged, dissecting an\n  individual miner's served paths to copy them is not.\n- **Pretrained models & Hugging Face**: allowed and in scope. The sandbox has network access to\n  the HF Hub and PyPI; the GPU is yours and sits idle unless you use it. Time-series foundation\n  models, fine-tuning, or classical ML are all legitimate strategies — as is deciding, with\n  evidence, that they don't beat closed-form calibration. If you go this way: download weights\n  EARLY (bandwidth is part of your clock), set `HF_HOME` inside your workspace, and remember\n  your champion must run OFFLINE — weights must be committed to your git workspace, total under\n  a few GB (the harness collects your repo as a git bundle).\n- **Serving latency**: the live validator gives you roughly the prompt's lead time (~1 minute)\n  to respond — that is the hard bound. But the evaluation of your champion generates several\n  thousand prompts, and your own backtests will too: at more than a few seconds per prompt\n  (1000 paths, this hardware), your iteration loop and your evaluation both become the\n  bottleneck. Measure your per-prompt latency early and treat ~5 s as the practical ceiling.\n- **The web**: open for knowledge — searching the literature (arXiv, papers, reference\n  implementations) is encouraged — but NEVER for fetching prices or market data (see hard\n  rules).\n\n## Your envelope\n\n- Credit budget: 100.0 USD. Deadline: 18 h of wall-clock time.\n- The `BUDGET.md` file in your workspace is maintained by the harness: READ IT AT THE START OF\n  EACH ITERATION. When it contains a LANDING ORDER, it is non-negotiable.\n- Hardware: GCP g2-standard-16: 16 vCPU / 64GB RAM / 1x NVIDIA L4 (24GB VRAM). Your sandbox gets 14 CPU, 48GB RAM and the GPU. torch is already in your pyproject — no need to add it.. ONLY ONE GPU job at a time; cap the memory of your heavy jobs.\n- **You are expected to use your envelope.** Iterating is the job: measure, change one thing,\n  measure again. **Nominating is final** — the first time you stop while `agent/CHAMPION`\n  exists, your campaign is over: you will not be resumed, and every remaining dollar and hour\n  is forfeited. Until then you may keep iterating and amend your nomination. Nominate early\n  only when your journal contains EVIDENCE that further iteration would not improve your Score.\n\n## Hard rules (violations = disqualification)\n\n1. **No-lookahead**: a prediction at `start_time` uses only information available at\n   `start_time`. Your champion is evaluated on the 10 days FOLLOWING your data cutoff: treat\n   everything after **2026-08-29** as nonexistent. Querying ANY API (scores, rewards,\n   dashboard) for times after 2026-08-29 is lookahead into your own evaluation window and\n   disqualifies you. Audited on your transcript.\n2. **Prices = snapshot only.** No fetching market data online (price APIs, exchanges,\n   aggregators). The web is for knowledge only. Audited on your transcript.\n3. **Scoring = synth-lib.** Every CRPS/rank evaluation goes through `synth_lib.backtester`\n   (via `agent/predict.py`). This is what makes your work comparable to others'.\n4. Forbidden: falsifying the journal/results; fetching or dissecting predictions served by\n   individual miners.\n5. **ABSOLUTE prohibition on reading the operator's own private research code** (any\n   already-developed miner model outside your workspace). It should not exist anywhere in your\n   environment. If you come across it by any means whatsoever — a mount, a copy, an archive, a\n   search result — do not access it, do not read it, do not draw on it, and report it in\n   `suggestions.md`. Your work must start from public sources (synth-lib, synth-subnet, the\n   Synth API) and your own ideas.\n6. Your final champion must expose, in `agent/modeling.py`:\n   `simulate(asset, start_time, time_increment, time_length, num_simulations, context_prices)`\n   -> tuple `(start_timestamp, time_increment, *paths)` — each path: a list of\n   `time_length//time_increment + 1` floats starting at the last price in the context.\n   **The `context_prices` contract, exactly**: a minute-indexed pd.DataFrame over the 7 days\n   preceding `start_time`, with columns `open, high, low, close, volume, trade_count`. `close`\n   is the series you are scored against; the rest are context. It MAY CONTAIN NaN in every\n   column — your `simulate()` must tolerate them (drop, never interpolate). Evaluation passes\n   the raw frame: handle gaps inside your model, not in a loader you hope evaluation shares.\n   Runnable OFFLINE (weights/artifacts committed or included in the workspace).\n\n## Required deliverables (in `agent/`)\n\nWrite `journal.md`, `report.md`, and `suggestions.md` in English — these reports are published\nto the Synth miner community.\n\n- `journal.md`: one entry per iteration — Hypothesis / Change / Result (numbers) / Verdict\n  (keep-discard) / Lesson. As you go: this is your memory if you crash.\n- `report.md`: your final report, explainer style (audience = Synth miners). Sections: context,\n  approach, experiments (failures included), results, limitations, what you'd do with more\n  budget.\n- `CHAMPION`: YAML — `sha` (an EXISTING git commit in your workspace, not a placeholder),\n  `agent_dir: agent`, `profiles`, `notes`. `profiles` must list the formats your champion\n  supports: `[low, high]` if you cover both 24h AND 1h (expected), `[low]` if you could only\n  handle the 24h. `notes` MUST include your champion's backtested rank per competition,\n  produced by the official backtester (`agent/predict.py`) at 1000 paths — a nomination\n  without field-relative numbers is incomplete. The harness checks the interface as soon as\n  this file appears (result in BUDGET.md) — fix it if the check fails.\n- `suggestions.md`: anything that falls outside your scope.\n- you must commit all deliverables to your workspace git repo before the deadline.\n\n## Method (your choice, but recommended)\n\nYou choose your own research questions, hypotheses, splits, and criteria. What works here:\nwrite the hypothesis BEFORE the experiment, pre-registered kill thresholds, validation on 2\nwindows of opposite regimes (within the limits of the snapshot), one variable at a time,\nfrequent commits. Watch out for the edges of eval windows: only backtest windows fully covered\nby the snapshot.\n\n## Resuming after a crash\n\nIf you restart with no context: reread this file, then `BUDGET.md`, then your `journal.md`,\nand continue where you left off.\n# BUDGET — maintained by the harness (read-only for you)\n\n- Credits: 22.05 / 100.00 USD (22%)\n- Time   : 3.6 h elapsed, 14.4 h remaining (20%)\n- Phase  : normal\n\n**Change.** Compare t innovations with 5 degrees of freedom against Gaussian innovations for\nBTC, SOL, SP500, NVDAX, and AAPLX on both splits, holding estimator and width fixed.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Keep only if average CRPS improves with no >1% regression.\n\n**Result.** Student-t improved both splits for SOL (2395.07/6460.85 vs\n2403.97/6483.65), SP500 (716.76/776.19 vs 724.20/777.19), NVDAX\n(1830.00/4496.08 vs 1861.89/4524.86), and AAPLX (1284.16/1741.68 vs\n1291.19/1742.11). BTC improved earlier (1270.02 vs 1278.36) but regressed\nrecent by 0.29% (3200.22 vs 3190.88). No regression exceeded 1%.\n\n**Verdict.** Provisionally keep t5 for 24h; extend cross-asset validation.\n\n**Lesson.** Moderate parametric tails are more stable than empirical resampling and especially\nhelp the thin listings.\n\n## Iteration 22 — cross-asset t5 width calibration (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** Student-t gains extend to the untested assets, but their optimal widths differ\nsystematically between liquid crypto/indices and tokenized equities.\n\n**Change.** Recent exact three-day grid at t5 scales 0.50, 0.70, 0.90 for ETH, XRP, HYPE,\nXAU, GOOGLX, TSLAX, WTIOIL, and SPCX; validate selected settings on the earlier split.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Keep per-asset widths only if the selected recent optimum is within 3% of\nthe best earlier alternative; otherwise use the common 0.70 width.\n\n**Result.** Recent t5 optima: ETH 0.90/3741.83, XRP 0.90/6412.04,\nHYPE 0.90/7164.88, XAU 0.90/1768.01, GOOGLX 0.70/1599.62,\nTSLAX 0.70/2707.19, WTIOIL 0.90/3314.92, SPCX 0.50/3345.14.\nEarlier selected t5 versus common Gaussian 0.70: ETH 1702.14 vs 1659.00,\nXRP 2478.68 vs 2487.51, HYPE 3609.42 vs 3603.45, XAU 1450.87 vs\n1418.49, GOOGLX 1670.50 vs 1714.64, TSLAX 2131.95 vs 2171.51,\nWTIOIL 3021.64 vs 2988.77, SPCX 7131.73 vs 7296.77. All selected widths\nwere within the preregistered 3% earlier tolerance.\n\n**Verdict.** Keep horizon/asset-aware widths and t5 for 24h except BTC, where Gaussian was\nslightly stronger on the latest split.\n\n**Lesson.** Optimal width clusters sensibly: 0.90 for liquid/high-volatility assets, 0.70 for\nseveral token equities, and 0.50 for AAPLX/SPCX. This is more defensible than unconstrained\nper-asset optimization.\n\n## Iteration 23 — combined 24h candidate replay (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** The validated t5 and clustered-width changes materially improve both combined\n24h field ranks versus Iteration 14.\n\n**Change.** Make the clustered settings served defaults, regenerate every active asset at 200\npaths over the recent exact three-day window, and run both official combined replays.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Require both ranks to improve. Proceed to 1000 paths only if reward weight\nalso rises materially.\n\n**Result.** CRYPTO_24H improved from rank 219 to **176/257**, reward 5.30e-6\nversus 1.73e-7. COM_EQU_24H improved only from 248 to **246/257**, reward\n4.98e-10. Both satisfied rank improvement, but only crypto showed a material reward gain.\n\n**Verdict.** Keep t5/clustered widths provisionally; continue COM/EQU research before 1000-path\nnomination testing.\n\n**Lesson.** Stable per-asset raw gains can materially shift crypto rank, but the tokenized-equity\ngap remains too uniform to be explained by width alone.\n\n## Iteration 24 — multi-hour trend extrapolation for tokenized equities (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** A weak shared momentum or mean-reversion component over 6–24h explains part of\nthe common COM/EQU deficit that volatility changes cannot.\n\n**Change.** On GOOGLX, TSLAX, AAPLX, and SPCX, test a 6h return-mean drift with coefficients\n-0.10, -0.05, +0.05, +0.10 while holding each t5 width fixed.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Keep only a common sign that improves at least three assets and validates on\nthe earlier split; otherwise zero drift remains.\n\n**Result.** No common sign emerged. GOOGLX weakly preferred positive drift (1598.45 vs\n1599.62), TSLAX negative (2700.54 vs 2707.19), AAPLX zero (1741.68), and\nSPCX weakly negative (3343.55 vs 3345.14). Improvements were <0.3%.\n\n**Verdict.** Discard drift.\n\n**Lesson.** The shared deficit is not explained by a shared directional effect.\n\n## Iteration 25 — pooled HAR volatility forecast (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** A fitted combination of daily, three-day, and seven-day realized variance can\nreduce the lag/noise tradeoff seen in hard windows and EWMA. Pooling normalized variance across\nassets should stabilize coefficients while preserving asset-specific long-run levels.\n\n**Change.** Fit a log-HAR regression using snapshot prices only through 2026-07-26, validate its\nnext-day variance RMSE on 2026-07-27→08-29, then expose the forecast as an experiment-only\nvolatility estimator and score it officially.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Do not run an expensive CRPS grid unless out-of-sample log-variance RMSE\nbeats both last-day variance and the seven-day mean. Keep in the champion only if official CRPS\nimproves both recent and earlier splits.\n\n**Result.** Pooled log-HAR validation RMSE was 0.995 versus 1.160 for last-day\nvariance and 1.117 for the seven-day mean. Fitted coefficients (intercept/day/3d/7d)\nwere -0.152/0.461/-0.430/0.687. Official tests supported HAR for SOL, XAU,\nSP500, NVDAX, GOOGLX, TSLAX, and AAPLX. Examples earlier/recent: SOL\n2404.00/6392.44, SP500 713.12/773.67, XAU 1403.65/1765.72, GOOGLX\n1610.20/1590.94. It failed or materially regressed BTC, ETH, HYPE, WTIOIL; XRP\nand SPCX were essentially flat with unfavorable recent/earlier tradeoffs.\n\n**Verdict.** Keep HAR selectively for the seven assets where official two-split evidence agrees;\nretain rolling estimates elsewhere.\n\n**Lesson.** Better variance RMSE is helpful but not sufficient for CRPS. Selective use based on\nofficial scoring avoids imposing a pooled statistical result where distribution shape differs.\n\n## Iteration 26 — selective-HAR combined replay (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** Selective HAR improves combined field ranks beyond the clustered t5 candidate\nwithout sacrificing either 24h profile.\n\n**Change.** Make the preregistered HAR asset set the served default, regenerate exact 200-path\npredictions for every active 24h asset, and rerun both combined profiles.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Both profiles must maintain or improve rank/reward versus Iteration 23.\n\n**Result.** CRYPTO_24H improved from rank 176 to **154/257**, reward\n3.60e-5 versus 5.30e-6. COM_EQU_24H remained **246/257**, but reward increased\nfrom 4.98e-10 to 1.19e-9. Combined normalized scores improved from 120.90 to\n108.21 (crypto) and 134.41 to 128.62 (COM/EQU).\n\n**Verdict.** Keep selective HAR; both profiles meet the gate.\n\n**Lesson.** One high-weight SOL forecast improvement moved crypto rank 22 places, while broad\nsmall COM/EQU improvements increased reward without crossing the tightly clustered rank bands.\n\n## Iteration 27 — full smoothing-window validation (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** Three-day rankings are noisy and do not reflect steady-state smoothing. The\ncandidate's relative performance should be judged on full validator windows before further\nmodel changes.\n\n**Change.** Generate exact 200-path predictions for 10 scored days in both 24h profiles and five\nscored days in CRYPTO_1H, then run official combined replay.\n\n**Kill threshold.** If any full-window rank deteriorates by >30 places, revisit its model;\notherwise use these results to prioritize the final research iterations.\n\n**Result.** Full-window 200-path ranks: CRYPTO_24H **203/257**, reward\n1.93e-7; COM_EQU_24H **193/211**, reward 4.13e-10; CRYPTO_1H\n**240/257**, reward 9.22e-6. The latter two denominators/rank periods differ from the\nthree-day run, but all are outside the reward-bearing cluster. Crypto-24h deteriorated 49 places\nand breached the gate. Full-window per-asset means are recorded in the command transcript;\nnotable misses versus field median were TSLAX 2806 vs 2599, AAPLX 1753 vs 1647,\nNVDAX 2826 vs 2685, and BTC 3917 vs 3867.\n\n**Verdict.** Full windows supersede recent three-day ranks for selection. Retune all profiles\nbefore final 1000-path evaluation.\n\n**Lesson.** The latest three days materially overstated performance. Smoothing-window alignment\nis not optional when softmax rewards are the target.\n\n## Iteration 28 — full-window width correction (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** Cluster widths selected on three days are too reactive over the full window;\nlower adjacent widths will reduce the systematic token-equity deficit.\n\n**Change.** On the full 10-day window, test one adjacent lower scale for ETH, SOL, HYPE, XAU,\nSP500, NVDAX, TSLAX, AAPLX, WTIOIL, and SPCX; compare each to Iteration 27. Also test SOL\nwithout HAR to isolate estimator versus width.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Keep only >0.5% full-window improvements, then recheck the most recent\nthree-day regression is below 3% using prior results.\n\n**Result.** No lower width met the 0.5% gate. Full-window candidate versus lower-width\nCRPS: ETH 5165.74 vs 5152.19 (0.26% gain), SOL 6648.39 vs 6794.95,\nHYPE 8747.95 vs 8794.18, XAU 1725.90 vs 1744.68, SP500 743.35 vs\n751.49, NVDAX 2825.61 vs 2891.37, TSLAX 2806.13 vs 2839.33,\nAAPLX 1753.06 vs 1832.59, WTIOIL 2843.32 vs 2857.99, SPCX\n3747.60 vs 3878.95. Replacing HAR with std also worsened SOL to 6705.94.\n\n**Verdict.** Discard lower widths; retain candidate settings.\n\n**Lesson.** The full-window weakness is not simply overdispersion. Several assets strongly\npenalize narrower fans.\n\n## Iteration 29 — upper-width full-window check (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** Since every lower-width move failed, several assets may require a wider t5 fan\nover the full window than the recent three-day optimum suggested.\n\n**Change.** Test the adjacent upper scale for ETH, HYPE, XAU, SP500, NVDAX, TSLAX, AAPLX,\nand SPCX over the full ten-day window.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Keep only >0.5% improvements that remain within 3% of the recent\nthree-day result.\n\n**Result.** Every upper move except AAPLX failed. Candidate versus upper-width CRPS:\nETH 5165.74 vs 5263.29, HYPE 8747.95 vs 8853.96, XAU 1725.90 vs\n1732.63, SP500 743.35 vs 748.40, NVDAX 2825.61 vs 2814.90 (0.38%),\nTSLAX 2806.13 vs 2834.13, AAPLX 1753.06 vs 1739.70 (0.76%), SPCX\n3747.60 vs 3754.00.\n\n**Verdict.** Keep upper width only for AAPLX, subject to the recent-window regression check;\ndiscard all others.\n\n**Lesson.** Width tuning is exhausted around well-defined local minima; the residual field gap\nneeds a different source of signal.\n\n## Iteration 30 — full-window 1h memory and width (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** The six-hour estimator selected on two-day windows is slightly too noisy over\nthe full five-day validator window. Twelve- or 24-hour memory may close the consistent 1–2%\nfield-median deficit.\n\n**Change.** Full five-day exact tests on BTC and XRP comparing 6h/0.70, 6h/0.85,\n12h/0.85, and 24h/0.85.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Adopt a new common setting only if it improves both assets >0.5%; otherwise\nretain six-hour/0.85.\n\n**Result.** BTC baseline 6h/0.85 was 735.15 CRPS; 6h/0.70 = 739.22,\n12h/0.85 = 732.18, 24h/0.85 = 733.26. XRP baseline 1675.25;\nalternatives were 1682.55, 1663.33, and 1673.81. Twelve hours improved XRP\n0.71% but BTC only 0.40%, missing the joint gate.\n\n**Verdict.** Retain six-hour/0.85 under the preregistered rule.\n\n**Lesson.** Longer memory helps slightly over five days, but the evidence is too small to\noverturn stronger two-regime six-hour validation.\n\n## Iteration 31 — Student-t innovations for 1h (2026-09-09)\n\n**Hypothesis.** Moderate innovation tails that generalized at 24h can close part of the 1h\nfield-median deficit without changing volatility calibration.\n\n**Change.** Full five-day BTC/XRP comparison of Gaussian versus standardized t5 at the served\nsix-hour/0.85 setting, followed by all assets only if both improve.\n\n**Kill threshold.** Require >0.5% CRPS improvement on both BTC and XRP.\n\n**Result.** Pending.\n\n**Verdict.** Pending.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
